{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues that apparent panel reliability is a base-rate artifact, but this is an argument about accuracy measurement, not reliability of judgments. The problem asks about 'reliable judgments' on tasks with 'objectively verifiable outcomes.' A panel that consistently produces the correct answer (even if aided by base-rate alignment) is definitionally producing more reliable judgments by the problem's outcome-based standard. SRC_001's counterconditions (balanced accuracy, minority-case discrimination) are evaluation-time observations about how to expose the artifact, but its core claim that the gain is 'merely apparent' presupposes that discrimination matters more than outcome correctness—which is the very question under debate, not a settled premise. The target's strongest counterexample—where the outcome distribution is not skewed and the panel's gain persists—actually undermines its own central claim by conceding the gain can be genuine in non-skewed settings.","counterexample":[{"balanced_dataset":"balanced_dataset","outcome_reliability_is_genuine":"outcome_reliability_is_genuine","panel_gain_persists":"panel_gain_persists"}],"target":"1ee841a3b809bf945e2d08e7df5e558f6706cb1f7472eb8aa3adf2653b946c9b"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's Condorcet/noise-cancellation mechanism rests on the premise that panelist judgments are 'independent draws,' but trained non-experts following the SAME explicit checklist are structurally correlated: the checklist is a shared deterministic function mapping each case's observable features to the same judgment. If two panelists apply identical checklist rules to the same case features, their errors are not independent idiosyncratic noise—they are shared systematic errors from checklist design (shared misreading, common training bias). The 1/√n variance reduction therefore does not apply. SRC_002's own counterconditions flag positive error correlation, yet its mechanism derivation still invokes Condorcet aggregation as the structural advantage, which is the false premise. The aggregation advantage is real only when errors are uncorrelated, which a common checklist actively undermines.","counterexample":[{"error_is_systematic_not_idiosyncratic":"error_is_systematic_not_idiosyncratic","panelists_1_and_2_both_misapply_rule_3b_identically":"panelists_1_and_2_both_misapply_rule_3b_identically","same_case_features":"same_case_features","same_checklist":"same_checklist"}],"target":"98c31b662a10088071bfb876326b126d45dfd96da50b02be722f46ce3bbd2b14"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's claim that the expert's intuition is 'an implicit, internalized checklist (latent variable model)' is an untestable assertion, not an empirical evaluation criterion. The problem specifically asks what evidence 'within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance.' By framing the expert's intuition as an implicit checklist, SRC_003 makes the comparison unfalsifiable within one evaluation: it cannot specify what observable evidence would confirm or disconfirm that the expert's implicit items are 'well-calibrated,' since they are latent and unobservable. The target sidesteps the problem's evidentiary standard by relying on an analogy that cannot be operationalized into an evaluation criterion.","counterexample":[{"evaluation_asks_for_observable_evidence":"evaluation_asks_for_observable_evidence","expert_internal_items_are_latent_and_unobservable":"expert_internal_items_are_latent_and_unobservable","no_single_evaluation_can_confirm_calibration_of_latent_cues":"no_single_evaluation_can_confirm_calibration_of_latent_cues"}],"target":"f3e44f625179973b66d9319d5979574733cf8da85bd80d29cc7c64304febf4d8"}]}